<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuyuhun</h1><p class="st-meta">329 CE – 670 CE (approx.) · capital Fuqi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom founded by Xianbei migrants on the Qinghai plateau that ruled the Qiang for three centuries and controlled a key trade route until Tibet conquered it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tuyuhun, an elder son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong Xianbei</a> chief, quarrelled with his brother and led his followers west in the late 3rd century, eventually settling around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Qinghai Lake</a>. His grandson Yeyan named the people after him around 329, establishing a state that ruled the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiang-historical-people">Qiang</a> herders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tuyuhun lived as herders famed for their horses, the Qinghai 'dragon colts', and dwelt in tents, though they built a walled capital at Fuqi west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Qinghai Lake</a>. They controlled the Qinghai route linking the southern Chinese dynasties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> spread among them. Kualü took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> in the 6th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> armies attacked in 609 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a> defeated them in 635, making the kingdom a Tang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> conquered it in 663, and the Tang defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river">Dafei River</a> in 670 ended hopes of restoration. Its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-nuohebo">Murong Nuohebo</a>, fled to Tang territory with his followers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tuyuhun.jpg" alt="Tuyuhun" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nirun,_Tuyuhun,_Yueban,_Tuoba_Wei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>329 CE – 670 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fuqi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kualü, Murong Fuyun, Murong Nuohebo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, steppe shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Azha, Tuyuhun Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=500&amp;focus=tuyuhun" aria-label="Tuyuhun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7176 844 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tuyuhun lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7496 1100L7713 1068M7496 1100L7771 1192M7496 1100L7771 1192M7496 1100L7372 1351"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7713 1068h0M7771 1192h0M7771 1192h0M7372 1351h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7496 1100h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7496 1100h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fuqi. <a href="/atlas?year=500&amp;focus=tuyuhun">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

